id: recipe.zero-downtime-deploy
kind: recipe
title: Zero-downtime deployment
aliases: zero downtime deploy, no downtime release, deploy without outage
question: how would you deploy a new version with no downtime?
tags: architecture, deployment, availability
properties:
  - capacity never below the required floor
  - traffic only to ready replicas
  - in-flight work drained, not dropped
  - schema changes compatible with both versions
  - a working rollback path
invariant: Keep serving while the version changes: overlap capacity, gate traffic on readiness, drain on termination, and keep old and new versions compatible during the window.
answer_30s: A rolling update with maxSurge/maxUnavailable, a readiness probe that reflects real serving ability, graceful shutdown with retries on the client side, and expand/contract migrations so both versions can run.
flow: |
  new image + template change
     |
  rollout: new ReplicaSet grows, old shrinks (surge/unavailable bounds)
     |
  gate: readiness probe -> endpoint list
     |
  drain: SIGTERM -> readiness off -> in-flight finish -> exit (grace period)
     |
  rollback: kubectl rollout undo (template only; schema must support it)
compose:
  - k8s.deployment.rolling-update => the mechanics of replacing the ReplicaSet
  - k8s.probe.readiness => the gate that keeps broken pods out of rotation
  - design.graceful-shutdown => the drain protocol on SIGTERM
  - k8s.resources.requests => surge capacity must fit on the cluster, or the rollout stalls
  - design.schema-migration => both versions share the schema during the window
  - design.health-checking => liveness versus readiness split so a blip is not a restart storm
  - design.timeouts => client retries so the endpoint-update window is invisible
  - k8s.rollback => a rehearsed plan for the bad case
  - k8s.events => observing the rollout's own failures (probe, capacity, image)
  - k8s.hpa => autoscaling interaction with surge capacity
failure_modes:
  - no readiness probe, so traffic reaches pods that cannot serve
  - no drain, so every deploy drops in-flight requests
  - grace period shorter than the drain, so SIGKILL interrupts writes
  - incompatible migration, so rollback is a rollback to a broken state
  - surge capacity the cluster cannot schedule, so the rollout stalls half way
follow_ups:
  - the new version fails after 10% of traffic moved -- walk me through recovery
related:
  - k8s.deployment.rolling-update
  - design.graceful-shutdown
  - design.schema-migration
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/#rolling-update-deployment
